{
 "ep": 13,
 "total": 140,
 "title": "Install PostgreSQL on Linux: Packages and Service",
 "phase": "Installation & Administration",
 "lines": [
  {
   "say": "From zero to a running PostgreSQL service.",
   "cap": "From zero to a *running* *PostgreSQL* service."
  },
  {
   "say": "This is episode thirteen of the PostgreSQL DBA series. Today: install PostgreSQL on Ubuntu from packages, then prove the service is running.",
   "cap": "Episode 13: *install* on Ubuntu, then *prove* it runs.",
   "cue": "prove"
  },
  {
   "say": "The PostgreSQL docs say binary packages are the recommended way to install, for most users.",
   "cap": "Docs: *binary* *packages* are the recommended way.",
   "cue": "recommended"
  },
  {
   "say": "Building from source is only recommended for people developing PostgreSQL or extensions.",
   "cap": "*Source* builds: for PostgreSQL and *extension* developers.",
   "cue": "source"
  },
  {
   "say": "Ubuntu includes PostgreSQL by default, in its own package archive.",
   "cap": "Ubuntu *includes* PostgreSQL by *default.*",
   "cue": "default"
  },
  {
   "say": "One command installs it: sudo apt install postgresql.",
   "cap": "*sudo apt install postgresql*",
   "cue": "command"
  },
  {
   "say": "But Ubuntu snapshots one major version for the life of that release.",
   "cap": "One *major* *version* per Ubuntu release.",
   "cue": "snapshots"
  },
  {
   "say": "On Ubuntu twenty four point zero four, that is PostgreSQL sixteen.",
   "cap": "Ubuntu 24.04 ships *PostgreSQL* *16.*",
   "cue": "sixteen"
  },
  {
   "say": "Want a different major version, like eighteen? Use the official PostgreSQL Apt repository, called PGDG.",
   "cap": "Other versions: the *PGDG* *Apt* repository.",
   "cue": "PGDG"
  },
  {
   "say": "The download page gives two commands to set it up. Then you install the package named postgresql eighteen.",
   "cap": "Set up the repo, then install *postgresql-18.*",
   "cue": "named"
  },
  {
   "say": "That repository plugs into your normal patch management, with updates for all supported versions.",
   "cap": "Updates arrive through *normal* *apt* *patching.*",
   "cue": "updates"
  },
  {
   "say": "Back on our test server. By default on Ubuntu, the package also creates a ready-made cluster called sixteen main.",
   "cap": "The package creates a cluster: *16/main.*",
   "cue": "cluster"
  },
  {
   "say": "Ubuntu's pg_lsclusters lists it: version sixteen, port five four three two. Output trimmed.",
   "cap": "*pg_lsclusters:* version 16, port *5432.*",
   "cue": "port"
  },
  {
   "say": "In this test container it was down, so we started it with sudo service postgresql start. Now it shows online.",
   "cap": "*service postgresql start,* then *online.*",
   "cue": "online"
  },
  {
   "say": "Next, pg_isready asks the server if it is accepting connections. Exit code zero means yes.",
   "cap": "*pg_isready:* exit code *0* means *accepting.*",
   "cue": "zero"
  },
  {
   "say": "Then connect as the postgres operating system user, and run SELECT version.",
   "cap": "Connect as *postgres,* run *SELECT version().*",
   "cue": "SELECT"
  },
  {
   "say": "We get PostgreSQL sixteen point fifteen, the Ubuntu build. Output trimmed.",
   "cap": "Running: *PostgreSQL* *16.15,* Ubuntu build.",
   "cue": "fifteen"
  },
  {
   "say": "Common mistake: assuming apt gives you the newest major version. It gives the one your distribution snapshotted.",
   "cap": "Myth: apt always gives the *newest* *major.*",
   "cue": "snapshotted"
  },
  {
   "say": "Another one: running the server as root. The docs say it must run under the PostgreSQL user account, and not by root.",
   "cap": "Never run the server as *root.*",
   "cue": "root"
  },
  {
   "say": "The docs say using the package's own startup tooling is much less work than doing it yourself.",
   "cap": "Use the *package's* own *startup* tooling.",
   "cue": "tooling"
  },
  {
   "say": "DBA takeaway: a common practice is to name the major version in the install, so every server gets the version you planned.",
   "cap": "Common practice: *name* the *major* *version.*",
   "cue": "planned"
  },
  {
   "say": "Recap: install from packages, and check which major version your source gives.",
   "cap": "Recap: *packages,* and check the *major.*",
   "cue": "check"
  },
  {
   "say": "Use PGDG for other versions. Verify with pg_lsclusters, pg_isready and SELECT version.",
   "cap": "*PGDG* for others. *Verify* three ways.",
   "cue": "Verify"
  },
  {
   "say": "Next up: initializing a PostgreSQL cluster, and what initdb really creates. Follow so you don't miss it, and send this to a friend who's learning PostgreSQL.",
   "cap": "Next up: *initdb* and the *cluster.* *Follow* and share."
  }
 ],
